PRODUCT ORACLE V2, WAVE 1, LANE v2-foundation. You are one of several lanes building Product Oracle v2 in parallel, each in its own SwarmCloud task and PR.

Read first, in this order:
1. docs/product-oracle-v2/build-prompt.md, the owner's build prompt: §0-§3, §12, §14, §15 and §16 in full, then your sections (§3, §4.2, §7, §9, §14).
2. docs/product-oracle-v2/waves.md: the wave plan, your lane's boundaries, the two hooks and the shared rules. Follow its shared rules exactly.
3. docs/questions.md: owner decisions, which take precedence over the build prompt. Where your work would conflict with one, do not override it. Build the compatible version and add a row to docs/product-oracle-v2/change-requests.md.
4. docs/build-brief.md (what exists and where), then the code you extend.

YOUR SCOPE: the shared foundation every other lane builds on. Inspect api/oracle/models.py, baseline/, docs/, review/, templates/, audit.py and the Alembic history first. Reuse existing models wherever one fits, and add only what §14 needs that does not exist.

1. ONE additive migration 0013 (down_revision = the current head; check it with `alembic heads`). Company-scoped tables, following the existing TenantScoped, permission and audit conventions, for:
   - semantic modules: definitions, versions (validated contract JSON, semver), template assignments (module manifests attached to templates), document compositions and composition revisions (modules with versions, inputs, decided_by, reason, status);
   - product intakes (short brief, author, pinned source snapshot ids, status, linked document);
   - knowledge gaps (category, impact, importance, evidence ids, confidence, status enum per §5, deciding owner, related modules, stop condition, links to baseline entities and section ids), stakeholder questions, stakeholder answers, assumption decisions;
   - blocker options, feasibility experiments and blocker resolution events, linked to the EXISTING baseline constraint entity (no new blocker entity if the baseline has one);
   - trace: new baseline edge or entity types where the baseline graph can carry them (§7: prefer one graph), plus document section links and artifact dependencies with a freshness state (fresh / potentially_stale / under_review / reconciled) and the causing change;
   - change events (the store behind oracle/changes.py);
   - evaluation reports and findings;
   - artifact requests, versions, outputs and review events (wave 2 uses them; create them now so wave 2 needs no foundation);
   - review correctness: section revision or hash on section verdicts and approvals, and an optimistic-concurrency version where a section can be edited.
   Do NOT add runs or swarm tables: the adapter v2 lane (CR-07) is changing them in parallel.
2. Pydantic schemas in oracle/v2/ for all of the above, including the module contract from §4.2 with every field it lists, and the applicability expression as a typed tree (AND/OR/NOT, presence, thresholds, typed comparisons; never code evaluation). Write validation only. The evaluator belongs to the modules lane.
3. The two hooks exactly as waves.md describes. oracle/changes.py: record_change() persists an event and awaits registered handlers. oracle/readiness.py: register_check(key, fn, hard) and evaluate(). Unit-test both with dummy handlers and checks.
4. Thin /v1 routes: list and get for each new entity (role guard, company scoping, negative cross-company test), so OpenAPI, MCP tools and the web client exist for the lanes to extend. Add no UI beyond regenerating the client and adding mock fixtures.
5. Legacy compatibility: every existing test passes unchanged. Old documents and templates without manifests load as before.
6. Write docs/product-oracle-v2/01-solution-architecture.md: the architecture of §3 as built on this codebase, each new table and why, how the hooks work, and how a lane adds schema (0014_<lane> re-chained at merge).

Finish: commit on your branch (tests first, then code), run everything the shared rules list, and write $SWARM_ARTIFACTS_DIR/v2-foundation-report.md as waves.md describes.

PRODUCT ORACLE V2, WAVE 1, LANE v2-audit. You are one of several lanes building Product Oracle v2 in parallel, each in its own SwarmCloud task and PR.

Read first, in this order:
1. docs/product-oracle-v2/build-prompt.md, the owner's build prompt: §0-§3, §12, §14, §15 and §16 in full, then your sections (§1, §15 Phase 0, §16).
2. docs/product-oracle-v2/waves.md: the wave plan, your lane's boundaries, the two hooks and the shared rules. Follow its shared rules exactly.
3. docs/questions.md: owner decisions, which take precedence over the build prompt. Where your work would conflict with one, do not override it. Build the compatible version and add a row to docs/product-oracle-v2/change-requests.md.
4. docs/build-brief.md (what exists and where), then the code you extend.

YOUR SCOPE: CR-00, the baseline and compatibility audit. It runs in parallel with the foundation step, so do not change application code. Add only docs and new regression tests.

1. Run every existing suite (make test, make lint, make openapi, web lint, tsc and build, Playwright against the mock) and record the exact results and any environment blockers.
2. Write docs/product-oracle-v2/00-baseline-and-delta.md as §1 asks:
   - the checked-out SHA against the pinned 869d3b7, and what changed since;
   - every capability classified working / tested / partial / spec / blocked from code and tests, not from descriptions;
   - what to reuse and extend (tables, routes, pages, suites);
   - decisions made and open;
   - migration risk and backward compatibility;
   - the SwarmCloud capability state as docs/swarmcloud-interface.md and the code show it (never claim live features).
   List every place where the build prompt conflicts with a docs/questions.md decision as a row in docs/product-oracle-v2/change-requests.md. Examples: the Head of Product chat against I4; anything touching O1c, O1g, W1, W2, W2b, P3.
3. Add regression tests protecting today's behaviour, in new files under api/tests/legacy/ (and web/e2e/legacy/ if needed):
   - legacy PRD and roadmap documents with {#id} headings, YAML resolutions, brief and full markers and the sources appendix;
   - import through API and CLI with no change in parse, diff or export;
   - company-edited and archived templates and themes untouched by upgrades;
   - locked sections;
   - roadmap provenance to an approved PRD version;
   - the role hierarchy viewer < commenter < approver < owner;
   - the >$50 approval and the >1.5x pause.
   These must pass on today's main.
4. Write docs/product-oracle-v2/06-security-and-threat-model.md: §12 against the code as it is. Cover IDOR and cross-company risks per route family, run tokens, source credentials, SSRF on public links, untrusted ingested content, and preview isolation (for wave 2). Mark each risk mitigated, partial or open, with the file and line.

Finish: commit on your branch (tests first, then code), run everything the shared rules list, and write $SWARM_ARTIFACTS_DIR/v2-audit-report.md as waves.md describes.
